## Chapter 1.3: The Charred Body

Luka pieced together his fragmented memory, trying to recall who is the person in picture. Finally he got an answer.

“Igor Romashin… Is he the director of logistics from Khabarovsk who was accused of embezzlement a few days ago?”

“Yup, exactly,” Nadezhda nodded then chuckled, “Heh, I hope you didn’t just use your vision just now. You do know quite a lot, Comrade Huo, maybe more than you should,” she teased.

Luka returned a smile before saying, “Well, to answer your question: No, I haven’t seen him.”

She nodded knowingly, already half-expecting the answer. While she could bid farewell right now, she hesitated. Internally, her professionalism and her instinct bickered. What she wanted to say was confidential but there was something about Luka that she found _special_. Perhaps, his intellectual authority made him more trust-able in her eyes.

“In that case, you’ve missed your chance…” She led him on, knowing that his curiosity would want to know more.

“What do you mean?” He replied, as expected.

She leaned in close, whispering, “If I tell you this, you have to keep it a secret, okay?”

“Uh… are you sure about this? If this is confidential, I rather—”

“Yes, it’s confidential and it’s unprofessional, but sooner or later you will hear about it anyway.”

Luka would want to refuse but his curiosity wouldn’t let him. What could have been so important that this officer wanted to break protocol to tell him?

“…Fine. Let’s hear it.”

Nadezhda took a moment to paraphrase the news. She said, “This morning, Igor Romashin was found dead in his sports car, burned to crisp. Right here in Pogranichny.”

Luka was taken aback, a high-profile fugitive ended up dead in this town? Burned to crisp, no less. One would no normally assume homicide which happened very rarely nowadays.

“…I— Wow. I mean, when did this happen?” Luka asked the only question that came through his mind.

“We don’t know for sure, but a citizen reported a fire around 4:30, I wasn’t on duty at the time,” which is a formal way of saying that she was sleeping, “the first responders along with firefighters arrived at the scene, put out the fire, and discovered the body.”

“But that’s all I heard,” she looks to the side with a disappointment in her eyes, “It sucks missing out all of the actions and sit quietly here.” At this points, she dropped all formality and spoke to Luka like a friend, “Imagine it, even a patrol officer might even got a sneak peak. Ugh, this is killing me, comrade. Out of all the time it could had happened, it just has to be on my shift…” she let out a big sigh.

Luka was caught in this awkwardness, he couldn’t get up and leave. She was venting about herself to him, not as an officer to citizen, but a comrade to a comrade. Though, he didn’t know how to comment. He had no clue how Militsiya works but perhaps, talking about shift, he might know a thing or two.

“Um… could someone cover a shift for you?” Luka quietly suggests.

“Covering my shift, huh…” Nadezhda pondered. That thought hadn’t cross her mind because she wouldn’t want to be penalized this early in her career. But if she had to do it, she might just had the right idea.

“Yeah. Yeah. I think that’s a great idea, Comrade Huo!” She quickly turned to her terminal, tapping fast on the keyboard and mouse. Her smirk widened every second, like a mastermind that saw their plan fits together. Luka could only sit there wondering if he had just enabled this officer to go rouge.

A moment later, she turned to Luka, on her hand was a rolled-up e-paper that she had just synced, “Now, comrade, we just have to wait for the final piece…” She smiled mischievously.

Luka raised his eyebrow in confusion, Nadezhda was definitely more of a wild card that he’d imagine. As he was about to asked what she meant, she cut him off,

“And… here comes the devil,” she leaned to the right to look pass Luka to the entrance. Luka looked back and saw a senior officer who just step through the door. As he turned back, Nadezhda said to him, “Ok, wait here a moment,” then she stood up and walked gleefully right over to greet the senior officer.

“Good morning, Uncle Senya!” She saluted with a bright smile.

A silver hair, senior officer probably in his late 40 to early 50, slowly took off his thick winter jacket, revealing a uniform underneath. An insignia on his shoulder only bear a think yellow vertical line that ran down the middle.

“To you to, Nadya,” he saluted back. He knew that attitude well, the over-activeness, the unnatural friendly smile, it was clear that she wanted something, “So… How can I help you, lass?”

“Have you been to the Igor’s crime scene, Uncle?” She asked.

Senya gazed upward, trying to remember, “Igor? Crime scene?” It seemed he was oblivious to the recent event.

“Oh. I thought you know,” she tried to explain to him concisely, “Well, Igor is a high-profile fugitive from Khabarovsk. We found him dead this morning, burned to crisp―”

“Woah, woah, stop right there! Don’t fill my head with gory image! It will ruin my morning tea time,” Senya stopped her story, he wasn’t very fond of death.

“Ah, sorry, Uncle,” she tilted her head slight then side, “but let me get to the point. Can you cover for me for an hour, Uncle?” She said with a pleading eye of the youth.

“Eh… but my shift starts at noon. What’s the occasion?” Senya hesitated, not wanting to agree based of pettiness.

“I haven’t seen a real crime scene before, Uncle. Please… I’ll buy something on the way back for you. Snacks, teas, fidget toys, whatever you want!” Nadezhda going full throttle on her plea. 

Senya let out a chuckle, “Alright, alright. A senior like me should support an aspiring youth after all. Just don’t take too long now.” he approved, “And also… get a me an oolong tea,” he smirked.

“Thank you, Uncle Senya!” She saluted him again and walked back to Luka.

Luka overheard the entire conversation. It seemed that he shouldn’t underestimate how scheming she was, given the chance, she would break every protocols if it gets what she set her mind to.

“That went better than expected,” she standing there, proud of what she had achieved, “Now, Comrade Huo, want to tag along? Provided that you are free from your academic duty,” she invited.

Luka thought, “_Oh great, now she’s just going to keep dragging me around like her partner-in-crime now_,” but a seed of curiosity was already planted inside him. Besides, what else he would do for the rest of the day anyway? He already called in sick at PIRM, his wormhole calculation was already done, and it’s not like he had a mood to go drink with Alex.

“…Well, since I already called in sick at PIRM…” He tried to sound reluctant, not wanting to look like a total pushover.

Her eyes lit up. Since Luka came, her day just kept getting better and better, “Perfect! Let me grab my winter coat.” She scuttled around the desk. 

Senya who just walked behind his side of the desk glanced briefly at Luka and then turned to Nadezhda and teased, “Well, well, Nadya, since when did you a boyfriend?”

Nadezhda rolled her eyes lightly and let out a small chuckle, “Ugh, Uncle, I’ve already you that I like woman, haven’t I?”

“I don’t know, maybe you realized that you’re bisexual, haha,” He laughed. Nadezhda shook her head lightly to dismiss his silly hypothesis.

During that time, Luka buttoned his winter jacket and getting ready to head out into the Siberian cold winter again.

“All set, comrade. Follow me!” She walked ahead to the door, leading the way.

“Good luck out there!” Senya shouted from behind the desk. Luka and Nadezhda looked back and nodded before exiting the precinct.






